    { w: "marcescence", pr: "mar-SESS-ens", pos: "noun", d: "The habit of keeping dead, dry leaves on the branch through winter instead of dropping them.", o: "From Latin marcescere, “to wither.” Young oaks and beeches are the classic examples; their brown leaves rattle on into spring." },
    { w: "anthocyanin", pr: "an-tho-SY-uh-nin", pos: "noun", d: "A red-to-purple pigment that many plants make in autumn leaves, berries and flowers.", o: "From Greek anthos, “flower,” and kyanos, “dark blue.” Sunny days and cool (not freezing) nights boost production, which is why some autumns are redder than others." },
    { w: "equinox", pr: "EE-kwi-noks", pos: "noun", d: "One of the two moments each year when the sun is directly over the equator, around 20 March and 22 September.", o: "From Latin aequinoctium, “equal night.” Day and night are close to equal everywhere on Earth, though not exactly." },
    { w: "crepuscular", pr: "kri-PUS-kyuh-ler", pos: "adjective", d: "Relating to twilight; describes animals most active at dawn and dusk, such as deer, rabbits and many moths.", o: "From Latin crepusculum, “twilight.” Compare diurnal (active by day) and nocturnal (active by night)." },
    { w: "gloaming", pr: "GLOH-ming", pos: "noun", d: "Evening twilight; dusk.", o: "From Old English glōmung, related to glow. It survived mainly in Scots and northern English, and poets kept it alive." },
    { w: "susurrus", pr: "soo-SUR-us", pos: "noun", d: "A soft whispering or rustling sound, like wind in dry leaves.", o: "Latin for “a whisper.” The word imitates its own sound, a figure of speech called onomatopoeia." },
    { w: "senescence", pr: "sih-NESS-ens", pos: "noun", d: "The process of aging in living things; in plants, the programmed breakdown of leaves before they fall.", o: "From Latin senescere, “to grow old,” related to senior and senate. Leaf senescence is orderly: the tree recovers nutrients before letting go." },
    { w: "Michaelmas", pr: "MIK-ul-mus", pos: "noun", d: "The feast of St Michael on 29 September, traditionally a quarter day when rents were due in England.", o: "Oxford and Cambridge still call their autumn term Michaelmas term. Folklore says not to pick blackberries after Michaelmas, because the devil, cast out of heaven, landed in a bramble and spoiled them." },
    { w: "St Martin’s summer", pr: "", pos: "noun", d: "A spell of mild, sunny weather in late autumn, around Martinmas on 11 November.", o: "A European name for the warm spell Americans often call Indian summer. Similar terms exist in many languages; in parts of central Europe it is called “old wives’ summer.”" },
    { w: "harvest moon", pr: "", pos: "noun", d: "The full moon closest to the September equinox.", o: "Around this time the moon rises only a little later each night than the night before, giving several evenings of bright moonlight right after sunset, once handy for bringing in crops." },
    { w: "scatter-hoarding", pr: "", pos: "noun", d: "Storing food in many small, separate hiding places instead of one large store.", o: "Gray squirrels, jays and nutcrackers scatter-hoard. Its opposite, larder-hoarding, is what red squirrels and chipmunks often do with one big cache." }
